WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.853
Two strategies so far.

00:00:04.853 --> 00:00:07.705
Locking makes you wait.

00:00:07.705 --> 00:00:10.558
Multiversion keeps versions so readers never block.

00:00:10.558 --> 00:00:13.411
The third is optimistic concurrency control, and it earns the name.

00:00:13.411 --> 00:00:16.264
Take no locks at all.

00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:19.116
Assume no one else touches your rows, run free, and check once, at commit.

00:00:19.116 --> 00:00:21.969
Usually you are right.

00:00:21.969 --> 00:00:24.653
Watch two transactions run with no locks.

00:00:24.653 --> 00:00:27.336
First, the read phase.

00:00:27.336 --> 00:00:30.020
T1 and T2 both reed what they need and compute on private copies.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.703
Nobody blocks.

00:00:32.703 --> 00:00:35.387
Second, the validate phase, at commit.

00:00:35.387 --> 00:00:38.070
One check.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.754
Did anything I reed get written by a transaction that committed since I started?

00:00:40.754 --> 00:00:43.438
T1 checks clean.

00:00:43.438 --> 00:00:46.121
T2 finds a row it reed was just written by T1, so it fails.

00:00:46.121 --> 00:00:48.805
Third, the write phase.

00:00:48.805 --> 00:00:51.488
T1 applies its writes and commits.

00:00:51.488 --> 00:00:54.172
T2 throws its work away and reruns.

00:00:54.172 --> 00:00:56.855
No one ever waited.

00:00:56.855 --> 00:00:59.539
T2 just ran twice.

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:04.864
So when does optimism pay?

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:10.189
When clashes are rare, almost nothing aborts and OCC runs lock-free.

00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:15.514
When the same hot rows clash again and again, transactions abort, retry, and burn work, and locking wins.

00:01:15.514 --> 00:01:18.495
Three strategies, one standard of correctness.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:21.477
Locking pays by waiting.

00:01:21.477 --> 00:01:24.458
Optimism pays by redoing.

00:01:24.458 --> 00:01:27.440
Multiversion skips the reader-writer wait with versions.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.421
Next, we connect all three into one transaction system.
